==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|huspaz||derision, mockery|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{proto|gem-pro|hut-||hūt-|to be naughty, be impudent|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|kūd-||to mock|lang=ine-pro}}. Related to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|hyspan||to mock, scorn, deride|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|husc|hūsc|mockery, derision, scorn, insult|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|hosc||vilification, ridicule, scorn|lang=goh}}.

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|g=m|pl=hospas|head=hosp}}

# [[reproach]], [[contempt]], [[contumely]], [[insult]], [[blasphemy]]

====Declension====
{{ang-decl-noun-a-m|hosp|}}

====Related terms====
* [[hospcwide]] m. insulting speech
* [[hospettan]] to ridicule
* [[hospspræc|hospsprǣċ]] f. jeer, taunt
* [[hospul]] contemptible
* [[hospword]] n. abusive language, contemptuous expression

====References====
* 1916, John R. Clark, "A Concise Anglo-Saxon Dictionary for the Use of Students", hosp.
* Bosworth, J. (2010, March 21). An Anglo-Saxon Dictionary Online (T. N. Toller & Others, Eds.). Hosp. Retrieved October 16, 2011, from http://bosworth.ff.cuni.cz/019576.

